* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - ask Windows instead of reading /etc/passwd, which
*           does not exist on the MSYS2 runtime.  See the description.
* rev 0.9.0 Jan 25 mab initial commit
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !is_user(username)
*
*    Is username a Windows local user?
*
*    This function sets the STATUS() value to 0 if successful, 1 if the
*    specified user does not exist.
*
*    WHY THIS NO LONGER READS /etc/passwd.  It opened /etc and read "passwd"
*    as a text file.  MSYS2 and Cygwin dropped /etc/passwd and /etc/group in
*    favour of direct SAM lookups, so the read failed and this returned false
*    for EVERY user, including ones plainly present - measured 14 Aug 2026,
*    with no /etc/passwd under either root the runtime can use, while
*    "getent passwd" answered correctly from the same shell.  The capability
*    was there; only the file was gone.  !is_grp_member had the same defect
*    and was fixed on 14 Aug 2026; !is_group and this are its siblings, found
*    later, and all three failed the same closed way.
*
*    WHAT IT DID TO CREATEA, AND WHY THE ANSWER WAS RIGHT FOR THE WRONG
*    REASON.  CREATEA asks this to find out whether the Windows account
*    already exists.  Answering false for everyone sent it to create_user(),
*    which fails on an existing account, so CREATE.ACCOUNT did refuse - which
*    is the rule (see below) - but reported it as "Create User Failed, OS
*    Error: 1", which reads like a defect rather than a decision.  Worse, the
*    refusal rested on a broken lookup: fixing this file alone would have
*    turned it into a silent adoption of somebody's existing login.  So the
*    rule was written into CREATEA in the same commit that fixed this.
*
*    THE RULE IT NOW SERVES, from the repository owner, 14 Aug 2026: the only
*    pre-existing OS user that may be given an SD account is the installer's,
*    and that is done at install time.  Every other SD account creates its own
*    OS account from within SD.  So a true answer here means CREATE.ACCOUNT
*    must refuse, and the one sanctioned exception is the internal-only ADOPT
*    keyword.
*
*    LOCAL ACCOUNTS ONLY.  Get-LocalUser does not see domain accounts, which
*    matches New-LocalUser in CREATE_USER and Get-LocalGroupMember in
*    !is_grp_member - the account model is local-account-based today.  A
*    domain user is reported as not existing, which on the refusal path is the
*    unsafe direction; noted in PROJECT_STATUS.md section 6.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function is_user(username)
$catalog !is_user

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

user_found = @false
set.status 0
* Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
* Empty username cannot match; avoid a pointless lookup.
if username = '' then
   set.status 1
   return user_found
end
* --------------------

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - the name is interpolated into the command below, so
*   it is validated first.  !valid_os_name permits letters, digits, dot,
*   underscore and hyphen only, which is what makes the quoting sufficient
*   rather than merely tidy.
if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
   set.status 1
   return user_found
end

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - SH1 names PowerShell, so the command IS the script.
*   -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue rather than try/catch: "no such user" is an
*   ordinary answer here, not an error.
   ps = 'if (Get-LocalUser -Name "' : username
   ps := '" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) { exit 0 }; exit 1'

   os.execute ps

   if os.error() = 0 then
      user_found = @true
   end else
      * No such user, or PowerShell could not be run at all.  Both answer
      * false, and the caller must treat that as "could not confirm" rather
      * than as "definitely absent" - see the description.
      set.status 1
   end

return user_found
end
